:       2. cite30†Metrics L41:       3. cite31†Comparisons L42:     3. cite32†4.3 Inference-Time Scaling L43:     4. cite33†4.4 Bridging Compression and Generation L44:       1. cite34†Generation with irrelevant prompts L45:   6. cite35†5 Conclusion L46:     1. cite36†Limitations L47:   7. cite37†References L48:   8. cite38†A Algorithm L49:   9. cite39†B Additional Experiments and Findings L50:     1. cite40†B.1 Distortion-Perception Trade-off via Early Stopping L51:     2. cite41†B.2 Distortion-Perception Trade-off via Early Stopping with Inference-time Scaling L52:     3. cite42†B.3 Additional Results on Editing L53:     4. cite43†B.4 Trade-off between Reconstruction and Memorization L54:   10. cite44†C Additional Backgrounds and Related Works L55:     1. cite45†C.1 Explicit + Implicit Representations for Visual Compression L56:     2. cite46†C.2 LoRA as Visual Memory L57:     3. cite47†C.3 Diffusion and Flow Matching L58:       1. cite48†Diffusion models. L59:       2. cite49†Flow matching. L60:       3. cite50†Conversion between ODE and SDE L61:       4. cite51†Equivalent between flow matching and diffusion model. L62:     4. cite52†C.4 Inference-time Scaling of Diffusion Models L63:     5. cite53†C.5 Reverse Channel Coding and Relative Entropy Coding L64:     6. cite54†C.6 Lossy Compression with Relative Entropy Coding and Diffusion L65:   11. cite55†D Proposition and Proofs L66:     1. cite56†D.1 Justification of the Overfitting Objective L67:     2. cite57†D.2 Bounds on the Distortion of Early Stopping L68:       1. cite58†(1) Error introduced by one-step map. L69:       2. cite59†(2) Error introduced by error in the vector field. L70:       3. cite60†(3) Putting them together. L71:   12. cite61†E Experimental Details L72:     1. cite62†E.1 Representation Experiments L73:     2. cite63†E.2 Compression Experiments L74:     3. cite64†E.3 Editing Experiments L75:   13. cite65†F Additional Results L76:     1. cite66†F.1 Full RD Curve L77:     2. cite67†F.2 Visualization of Compressed Videos L78: cite68†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org L79: \nL80: arXiv:2603.07615v1 [cs.LG] 08 Mar 2026\nL81: # Compression as Adaptation:\nL82: Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models\nL83: Jiajun He Affiliation: University of Cambridge Correspondence to: jh2383@cam.ac.uk    Zongyu Guo Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia Correspondence to: zongyuguo@microsoft.com    Zhaoyang Jia Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia    Xiaoyi Zhang Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia    Jiahao Li Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia    Xiao Li Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia    Bin Li Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia    José Miguel Hernández-Lobato Affiliation: University of Cambridge    Yan Lu Affiliation: Microsoft Research Asia\nL84: ###### Abstract\nL85: Modern visual generative models acquire rich visual knowledge through large-scale training, yet existing visual representations (such as pixels, latents, or tokens) remain external to the model and cannot directly exploit this knowledge for compact storage or reuse. In this work, we introduce a new visual representation framework that encodes a signal as a function, which is parametrized by low-rank adaptations attached to a frozen visual generative model.\nL86: Such implicit representations of visual signals, e.g., an 81-frame video, can further be hashed into a single compact vector, achieving strong perceptual video compression at extremely low bitrates. Beyond basic compression, the functional nature of this representation enables inference-time scaling and control, allowing additional refinement on the compression performance.\nL87: More broadly, as the implicit representations directly act as a function of the generation process, this suggests a unified framework bridging visual compression and generation.\nL88: ###### Keywords:\nL89: \nL90: Implicit Functional Representations, Visual Compression\nL91: \nL92: ^{†}^{†}affiliationnotice: Equal contribution\nL93: \nL94: cite69†website†compressionasadaptation.github.io L95: ## 1 Introduction\nL96: \nL97: Large-scale visual generative models (cite70†Esser et al., 2024 ; cite71†Brooks et al., 2024 ) have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in synthesizing and manipulating visual content by learning rich visual knowledge from massive data. With the progression from image to video synthesis, these models are further expected to leverage such visual knowledge for higher-level visual reasoning (cite72†Wiedemer et al., 2025 ), moving toward unified and generalist vision foundation models.\nL98: Despite this rich visual knowledge embedded in visual generative models, visual signals themselves remain external to the model. Visual content is typically represented as pixels (cite73†Meng et al., 2022 ), latent variables (cite74†Rombach et al., 2022 ) or tokens (cite75†Esser et al., 2021 ), which must be separately encoded and then fed into the model to enable downstream interactions such as editing.\nL99: This separation between internal model knowledge and external signal representations may lead to redundancy and inefficiency in representation, and limits the ability of the generative model to store or reuse past visual information over time.\nL100: To address this gap, we explore encoding visual signals as a function that describes the generation process, enabling synergy with large-scale generative models. This method—representing signals as a function rather than an explicit array—is commonly referred to as implicit (or functional) representations. An example is the implicit neural representations (INR).\nL101: They model visual signals as continuous coordinate-based functions, typically parameterized by small multi-layer perceptrons (cite76†Mildenhall et al., 2020 ; cite77†Sitzmann et al., 2020 ). While effective in compressing individual signals (cite78†Dupont et al., 2021 ), they are trained largely from scratch and remain decoupled from the rich knowledge learned by large-scale models across diverse data.\nL102: In this work, we propose to compress visual signals as model adaptations to large-scale diffusion generative models via parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) techniques (cite79†Mangrulkar et al., 2022 ), such as low-rank adaptation (LoRA) (cite80†Hu et al., 2022 ). While LoRA has been widely used for tasks like personalized generation (cite81†Ruiz et al., 2023 ; cite82†Kumari et al., 2023 ), we show that it also encodes implicit representations when optimized for specific visual content.\nL103: Leveraging the pretrained generative model as a visual knowledge prior, such implicit representations emphasize high-level visual semantics, making it particularly effective for compression. Moreover, we show that the induced adaptation parameters can be mapped into a single compact vector (cite83†Li et al., 2025 ), enabling compression of visual signals (such as an 81-frame video) into one vector.\nL104: cite84†Image: Refer to caption (a)\nL105: \nL106: cite85†Image: Refer to caption (b)\nL107: \nL108: cite86†Image: Refer to caption (c)\nL109: \nL110: Figure 1: (a) Explicit representations by encoding signals into symbolic latent variables. (b) Implicit representations that encode signal information implicitly in functions. (c) One-vector adaptation of large-scale generative models can serve as implicit visual representations.\nL111: We demonstrate that such implicit visual representations achieve strong perceptual video compression performance at extremely low bitrates on benchmarks, including the HEVC (cite87†Flynn et al., ) and UVG (cite88†Mercat et al., 2020 ) datasets, achieving high visual quality even under extreme bitrate constraints. Representing visual content as a function embedded in a generative model provides additional flexibility that is unavailable to conventional codecs.\nL112: In particular, the nature of functional representations enables inference-time control, allowing quality improvement through inference-time scaling. More broadly, the functional representations can be used beyond the reconstruction task and can serve as “visual memories” on specific identities, acting during the generation process, which suggests a step towards a unified framework to bridge compression and adaptive generation within pretrained generative foundation models.\nL113: In short, our core contributions include:\nL114: \nL115:   * •\nL116: \nL117: We describe a framework that represents a visual signal as a function that generates the signal. Such implicit representations naturally leverage the rich knowledge embedded in large-scale visual generative models.\nL118: \nL119:   * •\nL120: \nL121: We propose an approach that compresses the function into a single adaptation vector, achieving strong perceptual compression results on videos.\nL122: \nL123:   * •\nL124: We identify a key advantage of the functional representations: they can be flexibly controlled at inference time. Based on this insight, we introduce an inference-time scaling strategy for compression, which significantly improves reconstruction fidelity.\nL125: ## 2 Background\nL126: ### 2.1 Explicit and Implicit Representations\nL127: The distinction between explicit and implicit representations varies across research contexts. In some works such as implicit chain-of-thoughts (cite89†Deng et al., 2024 ; cite90†Zhu et al., 2025 ), latent activations from neural networks are regarded as implicit representations, emphasizing their lack of direct interpretability.\nL128: In contrast, much of the visual compression literature adopts a different perspective, where the distinction is defined by how signal information is represented: whether it is explicitly exposed as symbolic variables or implicitly encoded within a parametrized function (cite78†Dupont et al., 2021 ). In this paper, we follow the latter convention, where pixels, tokens, and latent variables are all treated as explicit representations.\nL129: #### Explicit representations for visual compression\nL130: Traditional lossy visual compression methods rely on explicit latent representations. A visual signal is encoded into a compact latent code, and reconstructed through a shared decoder. In this formulation, all sample-specific information is stored in the latent code, while the decoder remains fixed across signals.\nL131: This paradigm underlies neural codecs (cite91†Ballé et al., 2017 ; cite92†Cheng et al., 2020 ; cite93†Li et al., 2021 ; cite94†Guo et al., 2021 ) based on variational autoencoders (VAEs) (cite95†Kingma & Welling, 2014 ), where compression performance is governed by the expressiveness and entropy of the latent space.\nL132: #### Implicit representations as functions\nL133: \nL134: In this work, we use the term implicit representation to mean a functional encoding of a sample, i.e., representing a sample as a function. The function inputs could be coordinates (cite96†Stanley, 2007 ) or noise (cite97†Seeger, 2004 ), which are shared or signal-agnostic, without any semantic information about the signal itself. Figure cite98†1(a) and cite99†1(b) illustrate such differences.\nL135: One specific example of the implicit representation is implicit neural representation (INR), which parametrizes the function with a small neural network. Such INRs enable compact and continuous representations of images (cite78†Dupont et al., 2021 ), videos (cite100†Chen et al., 2021 ), and 3D scenes (cite101†Mildenhall et al., 2021 ), which have motivated recent exploration of INRs for visual compression.\nL136: However, existing INR-based compression methods (cite102†Guo et al., 2023 ; cite103†He et al., 2024a ; cite104†Kwan et al., 2024 ) typically rely on small standalone networks, limiting their ability to recognize or exploit the rich visual knowledge, even in some recent works where INRs are trained with diffusion process (cite105†Gao et al., 2025b ).\nL137: ### 2.2 Diffusions and Flows\nL138: \nL139: Our method builds on diffusion- and flow-based large-scale visual generative models (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 ; cite107†Wu et al., 2025 ). A diffusion model (cite108†Ho et al., 2020 ; cite109†Song et al., 2020 ) specifies a forward stochastic differential equation (SDE), gradually corrupting data to noise. We learn a score function by denoising score matching (cite110†Vincent, 2011 , DSM, ) to reverse the noising process, mapping noise back to data.\nL140: Many recent vision models (cite106†Wan et al., 2025 ; cite107†Wu et al., 2025 ) are instead trained under a flow matching formulation (cite111†Lipman et al., 2022 ). The model learns a time-dependent vector field $v(x_{t},t)$ and generates samples by solving an ordinary differential equation (ODE) defined by the vector field.\nL141: With the common flow-matching formulation, the flow ODE admits an equivalent diffusion variant that preserves the same marginal densities, and the vector field can be directly related to the score. We detail the connection and the conversion between these formulations in cite47†Section C.3 .\nL142: For the diffusion and flow-based vision foundation model, the generative procedure starts from random noise and then evolves the sample following the denoising SDE or the ODE. The samples they generated are determined by the entire dynamics, and hence, the generation process can be viewed as a representation of the generated sample.\nL143: ## 3 Methods\nL144: \nL145: This section presents our methods in details. We will begin with a high-level motivation and introduction to the framework of implicit visual representations.\nL146: \nL147: ### 3.1 Learning Implicit Visual Representations\nL148: #### Motivations\nL149: Our method starts from a simple observation: modern diffusion foundation models already contain a large amount of reusable visual knowledge in their weights, and they express this knowledge through a generation process. Strong priors about what “natural” images or videos look like are encoded in the pretrained weights and expressed through the entire generative procedure. Therefore, instead of compressing what the visual signal is, we compress how to generate the visual signal.\nL150: In other words, rather than representing a visual sample explicitly (as pixels, latents, or tokens), we seek implicit visual representation as specified generation functions defined on top of the foundation model.\nL151: #### Framework overview\nL152: Given a visual signal $x$, we first use vision-language model (VLM) such as GPT-5.1 (cite112†OpenAI, 2025 ) to get a detailed caption $c$. Then, conditioned on this caption $c$, we learn model adaptation parameters on top of a frozen large-scale visual generative model. As the caption $c$ is fixed for each video, in the following presentation, we will drop it for simplicity. The resulting learned adaptation parameters therefore constitute an implicit visual representation of the input signal.\nL153: #### Training objective\nL154: \nL155: We aim to fine-tune a diffusion (flow) model where the data distribution contains only $x$. Following the base models used in our experiments (Qwen and Wan; (cite107†Wu et al., 2025 ; cite106†Wan et al., 2025 )) which adopt a vector-field parametrization, we learn a time-dependent vector field $v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)$ with the flow-matching objective:\nL156:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathcal{L}_{\\text{FM}}(\\theta)=\\mathbb{E}_{t,\\epsilon}\\Big[\\big\\|v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)-(\\epsilon-x)\\big\\|^{2}\\Big],$  |  | (1)\nL157: \nL158: where $\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,I)$, and $t\\sim\\mathrm{Unif}[0,1]$. Here, $x_{t}=(1-t)x+t\\epsilon$ denotes the linear interpolant between $x$ and $\\epsilon$.\nL159: \nL160: After training, at generation time, we start from a random sample $x_{1}\\sim\\mathcal{N}(0,1)$, and either integrate the corresponding flow ODE or simulate the associated diffusion SDE:\nL161:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{d}x_{t}=v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)\\mathrm{d}t$  |  | (2)\nL162:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mathrm{d}x_{t}=\\left(\\frac{x_{t}}{1-t}+2v_{\\theta}(x_{t},t)\\right)\\mathrm{d}t+\\sqrt{\\frac{2t}{1-t}}\\mathrm{d}\\overleftarrow{w}_{t}$  |  | (3)\nL163: \nL164: where $\\mathrm{d}w_{t}$ represents the Wiener process (Brownian motion), and the arrow indicates the direction of the process. We derive the form of the SDE in Appendix cite47†C.3 .\nL165: #### Interpretation of the training objective\nL166: The training objective in cite113†Equation 1 aims to find a function reproducing $x$. However, such a function is generally not unique: many different generation functions can all lead to the same final reconstruction. This raises a natural question: which function should we prefer? For compression, we seek the simplest function that achieves the desired reconstruction, in the sense of deviating from the pretrained model as little as possible.\nL167: In fact, assuming the pretrained model and our model both follow the diffusion process (cite114†Equation 3 ), we can show our objective achieves the simplest function from the minimum description length (MDL) perspective.\nL168: A pretrained model induces a reference path measure $\\mathbb{P}$ on SDE trajectories in the path space $C([0,1],\\mathcal{X})$. Our new model induces another path measure $\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}$ on the same path space. The expected excess “codelength” incurred when we encode a sample from $\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}$ using $\\mathbb{P}$ as the coding distribution is the relative entropy $D_{\\text{KL}}[\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}\\|\\mathbb{P}]$.\nL169: Therefore, we want to find $\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}$ that deviates from the pretrained path measure $\\mathbb{P}$ as little as possible while enforcing the terminal state $x$. Let $\\{x_{t}\\}_{t\\in[0,1]}\\sim\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}$ denote a trajectory sampled from $\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}$, and write $x_{0}$ for its state at $t=0$, we want to solve:\nL170:  | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}}\\ D_{\\text{KL}}[\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}\\|\\mathbb{P}]\\quad\\text{s.t.}\\quad x_{0}=x.$  |  | (4)\nL171: The optimal solution^{1}^{1} 1 Conditioning on $x_{0}=x$ produces a measure $\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}$ that is singular w.r.t. $\\mathbb{P}$ on $C([0,1],\\mathbb{R}^{d})$, so the KL on the full interval $[0,1]$ is infinite. We therefore interpret $D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\mathbb{P}^{\\prime}\\|\\mathbb{P})$ as the KL in restricted path space on $(0,1]$ (equivalently, on $[\\tau,1]$ for $\\tau>0$ with $\\tau\\downarrow 0$).\nL172: is the base process $\\mathbb{P}$ conditioned on the terminal event $x_{0}=x$, which can be represented via a Doob’s-$h$ transform of $\\mathbb{P}$. Assuming the pretrained diffusion model is perfectly trained, the minimizer of cite113†Equation 1 recovers this solution. The proof follows directly from the definition of the Doob’s-$h$ transform together with Bayes’ rule; we include it in Appendix cite56†D.1 .\nL173: #### Benefit of functional representations\nL174: Functional representation brings two key benefits. First, it lets us directly leverage pretraining, as the compressed representation only needs to specify what is missing from the base model’s default generation process. Second, a functional representation is not a fixed, one-shot code: the function itself naturally acts as a prior and remains controllable even after encoding. This means we can steer or refine generation while keeping the representation itself unchanged.\nL175: ### 3.2 Compression with One-Vector Adaptions\nL176: \nL177: While the above objective arises from a KL-constraint perspective on the path measure, modern neural networks are highly over-parametrized. Therefore, in practice, we still need to apply an explicit rate constraint over the adaption.\nL178: #### One-vector adaption\nL179: \nL180: First, instead of directly fine-tune the entire network, we only train an adaption via Low-rank adaptations (LoRA) (cite80†Hu et al., 2022 ). For a pretrained weight matrix $\\mathbf{W}_{0}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times n}$, LoRA models the weight update as a low-rank decomposition\nL181: \nL182:  | $$\\Delta\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{B},$$  |  | (5)\nL183: where $\\mathbf{A}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{m\\times r}$ and $\\mathbf{B}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{r\\times n}$ with rank $r\\ll\\min(m,n)$. The new weight is given by $\\mathbf{W}=\\mathbf{W}_{0}+\\Delta\\mathbf{W}$.\nL184: Although the rank $r$ is typically small, modern visual generative models contain a large number of layers. Equipping each with its own pair of LoRA matrices $(\\mathbf{A},\\mathbf{B})$ keeps the total number of adaptation parameters substantial.\nL185: To further reduce the number of parameters, we draw inspiration from the hashing trick used in neural network compression (cite115†Chen et al., 2015 ; cite116†Havasi et al., 2019 ; cite117†Müller et al., 2022 ): instead of storing separate adaptation parameters for each layer, we map all LoRA parameters into a single shared vector through a fixed projection, randomly generated by a pseudo-random number generator (PRNG).\nL186: This design enforces parameter sharing across layers and encodes the entire adaptations into a compact vector $\\mathbf{v}\\in\\mathbb{R}^{1\\times k}$. Note that a closely related formulation has recently been proposed under the name Uni-LoRA (cite83†Li et al., 2025 ).\nL187: cite118†Image: Refer to caption Figure 2: Framework for visual compression with a single vector that adapts the diffusion foundation model.\nL188: #### Entropy constraint\nL189: \nL190: While the one-vector formulation significantly reduces the number of adaptation parameters, the vector represented in floating-point precision (e.g., 32-bit or 16-bit) remains inefficient for extreme compression. We hence impose an explicit entropy constraint on the vector representation $\\mathbf{v}$ and perform training-aware quantization.\nL191: Specifically, we (1) introduce a learnable scale parameter $s$ to normalize $\\mathbf{v}$ before quantization, and (2) apply uniform quantization using rounding, with additive uniform noise during training to enable differentiable rate estimation, following cite91†Ballé et al. (2017) . To model the bitrate, we adopt a factorized entropy model (cite91†Ballé et al., 2017 ) with learnable parameters $\\boldsymbol{\\psi}$, which assumes independent distributions over vector elements.\nL192: With this entropy-constrained formulation, the one-vector adaption can be quantized to approximately 1-3 bits per parameter while preserving good reconstruction quality. The additional parameters for the entropy model are transmitted in full precision: since the scale parameter is a scalar and entropy model relies on 8 distributional parameters (following cite119†Ballé et al. (2018) ), the overhead is negligible.\nL193: ### 3.3 Inference-Time Scaling and Control\nL194: \nL195: As motivated in cite13†Section 3.1 , representing a visual signal as a function provides an important benefit: after we obtain the one-vector adaptation, we can still control the generation process to enable modifications, improvements, or trade-offs, without changing the one-vector adaptations itself.\nL196: In this section, we highlight two representative directions that illustrate this benefit: (1) leveraging the idea of inference-time scaling, we can obtain substantial reconstruction gains while incurring negligible overhead in code length; (2) beyond compression, the adaptations can be repurposed as an instance-level memory, enabling downstream reuse for personalized generation.\nL197: Additionally, in Appendix cite40†B.1 , we demonstrate that by intentionally terminating the generation process early, we can achieve an inference-time distortion–perception (DP) trade-off for free. We now describe these two points in more detail.\nL198: #### Inference-time scaling with sampling\nL199: \nL200: Inference-time scaling (cite120†Ma et al., 2025 ) for diffusion models uses extra computation at decode time to improve sample quality. A common strategy is to branch the SDE trajectory by drawing multiple candidates at each denoising step, then apply selection procedures such as sequential Monte Carlo (SMC) (cite121†Wu et al., 2023 ; cite122†Singhal et al., 2025 ; cite123†He et al., 2025 ) or search (cite124†Zhang et al., 2025 ) to retain the most promising candidates.\nL201: When we compress data via a functional representation, these inference-time scaling ideas carry over seamlessly: after the encoder obtains the LoRA adaption, they can run the generation process using a pseudo-random number generator (PRNG) shared with the decoder. At every time step, the encoder samples multiple particles and then selects the most promising one. The selected particle is then encoded by its index using a small amount of side information.\nL202: The decoder can then deterministically reproduce the same chosen particle at every step using the adaption and the shared PRNG. Note that the scaling happens mostly on the encoding side, while decoding remains relatively fast; hence this can be viewed as encoding-time scaling.\nL203: Let us now make the algorithm more concrete to our setting. First, we now assume both the encoder and the decoder access to the vector field $v_{\\theta}$, parametrized by the pretrained generative model, plus our compressed adaption. Then, in order to branch the denoising trajectory with multiple particles, we adopt the SDE formulation in cite114†Equation 3 . We integrate the SDE numerically by Euler-Maruyama method.\nL204: Concretely, we discretize the entire time horizon in $N$ steps, with boundaries $t_{0}=0<t_{1}<\\cdots<t_{N}=1$ and step size $\\Delta t=|t_{n}-t_{n-1}|$. Each denoising step is given by a Gaussian transition kernel:\nL205:  |  | $\\displaystyle p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})=\\mathcal{N}(x_{t_{n-1}}|\\mu_{\\theta}(x_{t_{n}}),\\frac{2t_{n}}{1-t_{n}}\\Delta t)$  |  | (6)\nL206:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mu_{\\theta}(x_{t_{n}})=x_{t_{n}}-\\left(\\frac{x_{t_{n}}}{1-t_{n}}+2v_{\\theta}(x_{t_{n}},t_{n})\\right)\\Delta t$  |  | (7)\nL207: As this distribution is available to both the encoder and the decoder, the encoder can use the shared PRNG to draw multiple samples and select the best candidate. The remaining question is: what criteria should the encoder use to determine the best sample?\nL208: \nL209: To answer this, let’s recall the training objective in cite113†Equation 1 . Its minimizer actually enjoys an analytical form:\nL210: \nL211:  | $\\displaystyle v^{*}(x_{t},t)=\\epsilon-x{\\mathrel{\\to}^{x_{t}=(1-t)x+t\\epsilon}}{(x_{t}-x)}/{t}$  |  | (8)\nL212: For any input $x_{t}$ and $t$, the encoder can access this form, as it is then just a linear function of $x$. This means that the encoder actually access to the optimal denoising kernel:\nL213: \nL214:  |  | $\\displaystyle p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})=\\mathcal{N}(x_{t_{n-1}}|\\mu^{*}(x_{t_{n}}),\\frac{2t_{n}}{1-t_{n}}\\Delta t)$  |  | (9)\nL215:  |  | $\\displaystyle\\mu^{*}(x_{t_{n}})=x_{t_{n}}-\\left(\\frac{x_{t_{n}}}{1-t_{n}}+\\frac{2(x_{t_{n}}-x)}{t_{n}}\\right)\\Delta t$  |  | (10)\nL216: Therefore, the encoder can perform importance sampling, with $p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$ as the proposal and $p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$ as the target, to select the best particle. Precisely, we draw $M$ samples $x_{t_{n-1}}^{(1:M)}$ from $p(x_{t_{n-1}}|x_{t_{n}})$, and then select an index from a categorical distribution over ${1,\\dots,M}$, where each probability is proportional to the importance weight\nL217:  | $\\displaystyle w^{(m)}\\propto\\frac{p^{*}(x_{t_{n-1}}^{(m)}|x_{t_{n}})}{p(x_{t_{n-1}}^{(m)}|x_{t_{n}})}.$  |  | (11)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2603.07615v1] Compression as Adaptation: Implicit Visual Representation with Diffusion Foundation Models (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07615v1)\nciteturn32184view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.07615v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2603.23533] MDKeyChunker: What Does One LLM Call per Chunk Buy for Markdown Retrieval? (https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23533)\nciteturn32184view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.23533\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 169\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nraw.githubusercontent.com (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karpathy/autoresearch/500114a035cb63a4fc1829036e064b22870c61b4/program.md)\nciteturn32184view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/karpathy/autoresearch/500114a035cb63a4fc1829036e064b22870c61b4/program.md\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 16\n"
